Subject: [PATCH] arm64/fpsimd: SMSTOP only when streaming mode or ZA is on

With SME present, every syscall entry runs SMSTOP SM
(fpsimd_syscall_enter) and every kernel_neon_begin() runs SMSTOP
(fpsimd_flush_cpu_state), whether or not PSTATE.SM or PSTATE.ZA is set.
On Apple M4 each SMSTOP slows the core by about 40 % for some 20 us
afterwards, even when it has nothing to stop, so a guest that sees SME
runs syscall-heavy work 8-43 % slower and its NEON crypto 25-30 % slower.
Reading SVCR first costs nothing measurable, and SMSTOP changes nothing
when both bits are already clear. Only these two helpers change; a task
that uses streaming mode or ZA is stopped exactly as before.

Evidence: docs/experiments/2026-10-14-guest-crypto-and-smstop.md.

--- a/arch/arm64/include/asm/fpsimd.h
+++ b/arch/arm64/include/asm/fpsimd.h
@@ -380,14 +380,23 @@
 	asm volatile(__msr_s(SYS_SVCR_SMSTART_SM_EL0, "xzr"));
 }
 
+/*
+ * vmuc: stop only what is on. On Apple M4 an SMSTOP slows the core by
+ * about 40 % for some 20 us even when PSTATE.SM and PSTATE.ZA are already
+ * clear, and these run on every syscall entry and every
+ * kernel_neon_begin(). Reading SVCR costs nothing measurable, and an
+ * SMSTOP that changes neither bit has no architectural effect.
+ */
 static inline void sme_smstop_sm(void)
 {
-	asm volatile(__msr_s(SYS_SVCR_SMSTOP_SM_EL0, "xzr"));
+	if (read_sysreg_s(SYS_SVCR) & SVCR_SM_MASK)
+		asm volatile(__msr_s(SYS_SVCR_SMSTOP_SM_EL0, "xzr"));
 }
 
 static inline void sme_smstop(void)
 {
-	asm volatile(__msr_s(SYS_SVCR_SMSTOP_SMZA_EL0, "xzr"));
+	if (read_sysreg_s(SYS_SVCR) & (SVCR_SM_MASK | SVCR_ZA_MASK))
+		asm volatile(__msr_s(SYS_SVCR_SMSTOP_SMZA_EL0, "xzr"));
 }
 
 extern void __init sme_setup(void);
